\subsection{Low-wave noise and directional observability}
\label{sec:low-wave-noise-weighting}

The known vessel response suppresses horizontal wave acceleration toward the
sensor noise floor. A bounded measurement weight uses the preceding
measurement-only wave-band scale and frequency to predict, for horizontal
sensor axis $i$,
\begin{equation}
 \hat\sigma_i=\sigma_z\,
 \frac{\sqrt{(1-(fT_h)^2)^2+(2\zeta_h fT_h)^2}}
 {1+(2\pi f\tau_i)^2},\qquad
 \rho_i=\hat\sigma_i/\sigma_{a,i}.
\end{equation}
The unknown incident-direction projection is bounded by one, and common
depth/footprint attenuation is omitted conservatively. The accelerometer
standard-deviation multiplier is four for $\rho_i\le2$ and exactly one for
$\rho_i\ge4$; its variance multiplier follows a cubic smoothstep between
those endpoints. Vertical weighting is unchanged. Vibration inflation is
added in variance after this weight. The current measurement cannot select
its own covariance. This is an empirical regularization policy for the
configured stationary hull, not an estimate of injected sensor-noise density.
It is independent of the direction-equalizer switch and leaves the OU prior,
$\tau$, $R_S$ law, and acceptance thresholds unchanged.

Four fresh paired sensor/initialization draws (31013, 37003, 41011, 47017)
on all eight records give the following results. Acceleration columns average
only the two lowest-wave records across those draws; the remaining columns
average all 32 cases. The fixed prescribed sea records do not constitute
independent sea or hull realizations.
\begin{table*}[t]\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Fresh-draw low-wave weighting validation. Acceleration is in
\si{m.s^{-2}}, attitude in degrees. Counts are unchanged-threshold violations,
not failing records.}
\label{tab:low-wave-noise-weighting}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrrr@{}}\toprule
Family & Weighting & Low $a_x$ RMS & Low $a_y$ RMS & Roll & Pitch & Yaw & Violations/32\\
\midrule
OU--II & Off & 0.009421 & 0.009486 & 0.2553 & 0.3282 & 1.9230 & 70\\
OU--II & Response/SNR & 0.006875 & 0.006750 & 0.2551 & 0.3305 & 1.9282 & 69\\
OU--III & Off & 0.007941 & 0.007976 & 0.2142 & 0.3272 & 1.9234 & 81\\
OU--III & Response/SNR & 0.006370 & 0.006081 & 0.2128 & 0.3308 & 1.9334 & 80\\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table*}
OU--II still fails 30 of 32 cases and OU--III all 32. Mean pitch and yaw
increase slightly; the improvement is principally acceleration noise, with
OU--III low-wave 3-D displacement RMS decreasing from \SI{0.02121}{m} to
\SI{0.01753}{m}. New-angle and rotated-heading paired checks add no
violations but retain small attitude tradeoffs.

The direction input subtracts the estimated temperature-dependent
accelerometer bias in physical sensor axes before levelling and response
matching. The separate wave-period/tuning input remains measurement-only.
An unavailable zero axis produces no angular score. At the default lowest
waves, corrected axis availability averages about one percent and travel direction
is unresolved more than 99 percent of the time. Conditional angular accuracy
therefore does not establish usable low-wave direction. Static tilt/bias
ambiguity and incomplete magnetic calibration remain limiting mechanisms;
no new yaw or $R_S$ default is justified by the tuning screens.
